WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.450 --> 00:00:09.450
To me a good tattoo is a
clean outline, smooth shading,

00:00:10.410 --> 00:00:12.910
if it's color, solid color,

00:00:12.910 --> 00:00:14.703
and originality.

00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:18.610
What makes a good tattoo changes.

00:00:18.610 --> 00:00:20.280
If you have lighter skin,

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:23.730
you can almost do a tattoo
just as you would a painting.

00:00:23.730 --> 00:00:25.350
But if you have darker skin,

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:28.653
then you need to simplify
it, so it can be seen.

00:00:30.140 --> 00:00:30.973
When it come to tattooing,

00:00:30.973 --> 00:00:33.470
I'm just a vessel of that creativity.

00:00:33.470 --> 00:00:36.120
I'm just sitting back,
watching my hand move.

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.190
I'm not thinking about it.
I know it ain't me no more.

00:00:40.194 --> 00:00:42.650
It's creation, it's
God, it's another place.

00:00:42.650 --> 00:00:46.000
And I don't care what the
complexion of the person is.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:47.863
I'm gonna make them something beautiful.

00:00:56.810 --> 00:00:58.503
Who is Miya Bailey?

00:00:59.580 --> 00:01:02.990
I would probably say an
artist, community builder,

00:01:02.990 --> 00:01:03.823
team builder.

00:01:07.810 --> 00:01:10.520
I would describe Peters
Street Station first as an art

00:01:10.520 --> 00:01:13.113
and design community center,
down here in Atlanta.

00:01:14.670 --> 00:01:18.780
We got tattoo artists, our painters,
our writers, our musicians,

00:01:18.780 --> 00:01:20.280
our singers, under one roof,

00:01:20.280 --> 00:01:21.630
and we feed off each other.

00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.100
And my whole goal here is
to create new styles of art

00:01:26.100 --> 00:01:30.910
and let these younger
artists shine, train them,

00:01:30.910 --> 00:01:34.270
make sure I got full
attention on their resources.

00:01:34.270 --> 00:01:35.103
My whole goal is to see them

00:01:35.103 --> 00:01:36.773
just go as far as they wanna go.

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:41.790
Right now, I shall lead
you into the humble abode.

00:01:41.790 --> 00:01:43.073
When people come in

00:01:43.073 --> 00:01:44.380
and they're getting a tattoo,

00:01:44.380 --> 00:01:46.270
we give them a tour of the building.

00:01:46.270 --> 00:01:48.970
Miya has his private art
collection hanging up here.

00:01:48.970 --> 00:01:51.200
My whole goal is to make
sure that people come here

00:01:51.200 --> 00:01:52.410
and feel welcomed.

00:01:52.410 --> 00:01:54.180
So this is Miya's station.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:56.450
This is where you will
be getting tattooed.

00:01:56.450 --> 00:01:58.670
I want them be excited too,
'cause when they excited,

00:01:58.670 --> 00:01:59.743
it make me excited.

00:02:04.370 --> 00:02:06.730
When the client get here,
I'm not gonna be here,

00:02:06.730 --> 00:02:08.093
but I got apprentices.

00:02:10.403 --> 00:02:12.301
And what they're supposed

00:02:12.301 --> 00:02:15.624
to do is get this client comfortable.

00:02:15.624 --> 00:02:17.580
Nice to meet you.

00:02:17.580 --> 00:02:19.100
All right so what're you getting today?

00:02:19.100 --> 00:02:19.933
I don't know.

00:02:19.933 --> 00:02:21.180
You don't know?
No.

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:23.690
There are a couple of elements
I would like to include.

00:02:23.690 --> 00:02:25.793
Like, I would like an Om.
That's very important to me.

00:02:25.793 --> 00:02:28.430
Like, it's a very big part of who

00:02:28.430 --> 00:02:29.620
and what I am.

00:02:29.620 --> 00:02:32.720
Before I get to work, you
know, the first thing I do,

00:02:32.720 --> 00:02:35.184
I make sure I got some alone time.

00:02:35.184 --> 00:02:37.730
Take a long walk.

00:02:37.730 --> 00:02:40.520
I kind of take as much
time as I can with my dogs,

00:02:40.520 --> 00:02:42.373
enjoy that, watch them play around.

00:02:44.680 --> 00:02:46.690
I really truly don't know
exactly what I'm gonna do

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:48.310
till I get there.

00:02:48.310 --> 00:02:51.447
Good to meet you.
You as well.

00:02:51.447 --> 00:02:53.210
Can you tell me your concept today?

00:02:53.210 --> 00:02:56.210
So, I'm a tattoo artist,
Yeah.

00:02:56.210 --> 00:02:57.110
right?

00:02:57.110 --> 00:02:58.960
And you are my inspiration for being one.

00:02:58.960 --> 00:03:00.650
Oh thank you man, damn.

00:03:00.650 --> 00:03:02.570
I would love anything, your style.

00:03:02.570 --> 00:03:03.480
I like bright colors

00:03:03.480 --> 00:03:04.730
and I love cartoons.

00:03:04.730 --> 00:03:06.530
If you can like, your
favorite cartoon character-

00:03:06.530 --> 00:03:07.700
Simpsons.
"The Simpsons"?

00:03:07.700 --> 00:03:08.750
Yes.

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:10.670
When I meet the person for the first time,

00:03:10.670 --> 00:03:13.030
I kind of just let them do their talking,

00:03:13.030 --> 00:03:13.863
you know what I'm saying?

00:03:13.863 --> 00:03:15.920
Like, when they explain
it to me face to face,

00:03:15.920 --> 00:03:17.030
I can feel it.

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:20.070
I like abstract work. I like masks.

00:03:20.070 --> 00:03:23.490
I have a lot of people in
healthcare in my family,

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:26.110
so I wanted to kind of
just give a little tribute

00:03:26.110 --> 00:03:28.970
to the healers in my family.

00:03:28.970 --> 00:03:30.150
Okay. Okay.

00:03:30.150 --> 00:03:32.027
Once they start reading
out the story it's like,

00:03:32.027 --> 00:03:33.597
"okay, either can be family based

00:03:33.597 --> 00:03:38.597
or trauma, or overcoming,
or spiritual growth."

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:42.180
When I was telling my
wife about coming here

00:03:42.180 --> 00:03:44.340
and like how this was,

00:03:44.340 --> 00:03:46.205
I don't wanna say it's a
religious experience for me

00:03:46.205 --> 00:03:47.650
but it is.
Yeah.

00:03:47.650 --> 00:03:48.560
You know what I mean?

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:51.353
And so I just want that pouring into this.

00:03:57.560 --> 00:03:59.050
Before we can start step one,

00:03:59.050 --> 00:04:01.353
my apprentices, they prep the whole area.

00:04:02.760 --> 00:04:05.620
They set up my inks in certain orders,

00:04:05.620 --> 00:04:07.103
through darks to lights.

00:04:09.170 --> 00:04:11.520
Then they gonna shave the arm

00:04:11.520 --> 00:04:14.220
and just make sure that the
skin is completely smooth

00:04:14.220 --> 00:04:15.070
without hair.

00:04:15.070 --> 00:04:18.290
You don't want a needle
jagging up in a piece of hair.

00:04:18.290 --> 00:04:19.417
Or you run over a piece of hair

00:04:19.417 --> 00:04:22.510
and you mess up your
line, or getting tangled

00:04:22.510 --> 00:04:23.950
and it pulls the hair out,

00:04:23.950 --> 00:04:25.800
that hurts, you know what I'm saying?

00:04:26.990 --> 00:04:29.350
The next step is to freehand work.

00:04:29.350 --> 00:04:34.250
I get my red marker
first. Red is just sketch.

00:04:34.250 --> 00:04:36.900
So you'll see me sketch with the red,

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:39.040
get a nice rhythm going,

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:40.400
then I take a black marker,

00:04:40.400 --> 00:04:41.830
and then I do the black line,

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:44.530
that's the actual line that
the client's gonna see.

00:04:44.530 --> 00:04:46.170
That's when I'm showing
the actual designs,

00:04:46.170 --> 00:04:48.103
my guidelines, the black lines.

00:04:48.950 --> 00:04:52.980
When I use straight lines,
that means I'm thinking logic,

00:04:52.980 --> 00:04:55.850
thinking thoughts, but
anytime you see curves

00:04:55.850 --> 00:04:58.833
and movement in it, it
represents love or emotion.

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:01.910
After I finish doing the black ink,

00:05:01.910 --> 00:05:03.350
then that's when, you know,

00:05:03.350 --> 00:05:05.280
I'm kind of finished the design.

00:05:05.280 --> 00:05:08.210
Let the clients stand
up, look in the mirror.

00:05:08.210 --> 00:05:11.740
Usually they like it. I just
wait for that expression.

00:05:11.740 --> 00:05:14.300
And once I get that
expression on their face,

00:05:14.300 --> 00:05:17.693
then I know that it's
gonna be a good session.

00:05:25.400 --> 00:05:28.400
Increasingly the institutional
art world is honing in

00:05:28.400 --> 00:05:30.543
on tattooing as being a kind of art.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:33.010
And we've seen people

00:05:33.010 --> 00:05:34.860
that have completed their art degrees,

00:05:34.860 --> 00:05:36.123
moving to tattooing.

00:05:37.240 --> 00:05:38.200
I'm David Lane.

00:05:38.200 --> 00:05:41.160
I'm an assistant professor
of Criminal Justice Sciences

00:05:41.160 --> 00:05:42.913
at Illinois State University.

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:48.720
Part of what I study is crime

00:05:48.720 --> 00:05:49.553
and deviance,

00:05:49.553 --> 00:05:50.940
and the other part is culture

00:05:50.940 --> 00:05:51.773
and art.

00:05:55.140 --> 00:05:55.973
The crime

00:05:55.973 --> 00:05:56.806
and deviance pays the bills,

00:05:56.806 --> 00:06:00.083
but I get to do the art
part when I study tattooing.

00:06:04.850 --> 00:06:08.720
The oldest human bodies ever
recovered have tattoos on them,

00:06:08.720 --> 00:06:09.670
meaning that we know it's

00:06:09.670 --> 00:06:12.580
at least a 5,000 year old practice.

00:06:12.580 --> 00:06:15.140
There's not a single tribal
society in the world,

00:06:15.140 --> 00:06:18.400
in which the indigenous people
don't practice tattooing.

00:06:18.400 --> 00:06:20.120
We can go to New Zealand, historically,

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:21.580
and the Mallory tribes.

00:06:21.580 --> 00:06:24.140
Often their face documented
a lot of things like

00:06:24.140 --> 00:06:26.570
whether people were on their
first or second marriage,

00:06:26.570 --> 00:06:29.430
what kind of labor they carried
out, their family lineage,

00:06:29.430 --> 00:06:31.660
all sorts of things could
be read off the face.

00:06:31.660 --> 00:06:34.110
Here in what's now the United States,

00:06:34.110 --> 00:06:37.120
we have documentation that
Alaskan natives tattooed.

00:06:37.120 --> 00:06:39.350
People in the Southwest tattoo themselves,

00:06:39.350 --> 00:06:43.700
even in Tennessee, we found
2000 year old tattoo needles.

00:06:43.700 --> 00:06:45.000
And then ultimately,

00:06:45.000 --> 00:06:47.260
if we think about our
contemporary context,

00:06:47.260 --> 00:06:49.040
tattooing, it's more popular

00:06:49.040 --> 00:06:51.240
and accessible than it
ever has been before.

00:06:53.260 --> 00:06:55.330
There were a few sort
of, pop culture things

00:06:55.330 --> 00:06:57.980
that all sort of, coincided
over a number of decades.

00:07:01.530 --> 00:07:03.250
You had pop stars getting tattooed

00:07:03.250 --> 00:07:04.510
and by the 1980s,

00:07:04.510 --> 00:07:06.060
and definitely by the '90s,

00:07:06.060 --> 00:07:07.520
you're starting to see athletes,

00:07:07.520 --> 00:07:10.900
whether it's football or NBA,
they're visibly tattooed.

00:07:10.900 --> 00:07:13.270
By the time we get into the mid-2000's,

00:07:13.270 --> 00:07:15.540
we sort of have the birth of social media

00:07:15.540 --> 00:07:17.270
and that allows people to take images

00:07:17.270 --> 00:07:18.560
of their own tattoos,

00:07:18.560 --> 00:07:20.670
or take images of tattoos they produced

00:07:20.670 --> 00:07:22.793
and spread it to new audiences easier.

00:07:24.080 --> 00:07:25.980
When I first started doing this research,

00:07:25.980 --> 00:07:28.170
and this is over a decade ago,

00:07:28.170 --> 00:07:31.820
Jacci Gresham was one of the
first names that ever came up.

00:07:31.820 --> 00:07:34.310
The conversation around Jacci,

00:07:34.310 --> 00:07:38.350
it can be summed up in a
couple words: original,

00:07:38.350 --> 00:07:39.423
and groundbreaking.

00:07:42.310 --> 00:07:43.710
My name is Jacci Gresham,

00:07:43.710 --> 00:07:46.713
and I've been in New Orleans
for 45 years tattooing.

00:07:49.940 --> 00:07:53.463
I'm the first Black woman to tattoo.

00:07:55.730 --> 00:07:58.663
This shop, back in the '70s

00:07:58.663 --> 00:08:02.150
and '80s, we had bike clubs
come in here every Sunday.

00:08:02.150 --> 00:08:03.790
They would come in here.

00:08:03.790 --> 00:08:07.983
And back then, that was
pretty much your clientele.

00:08:09.600 --> 00:08:11.217
Jackie was doing imagery

00:08:11.217 --> 00:08:13.870
that was an aesthetic challenge to

00:08:13.870 --> 00:08:16.610
what some white tattooers
had considered the sort of,

00:08:16.610 --> 00:08:18.200
essence of tattooing.

00:08:18.200 --> 00:08:20.690
So she sort of broke in
ground in that sense.

00:08:20.690 --> 00:08:25.150
Today, tattoos, the
different types are endless.

00:08:25.150 --> 00:08:28.023
If you can see it, we
can do it as a tattoo.

00:08:29.020 --> 00:08:31.380
Ms. Jacci, the world
should know who she is,

00:08:31.380 --> 00:08:32.990
in my opinion.

00:08:32.990 --> 00:08:36.740
She's special. Imagine
what she had to deal with.

00:08:36.740 --> 00:08:37.853
I came up in the '90s,

00:08:37.853 --> 00:08:40.300
and it was super racist.

00:08:40.300 --> 00:08:43.520
So I couldn't imagine the
things she had to go through.

00:08:43.520 --> 00:08:47.540
I think it makes it harder
that I was a Black person.

00:08:47.540 --> 00:08:52.540
I think also women in the
industry have a hard time as well.

00:08:52.570 --> 00:08:54.047
When I wanted to buy this building,

00:08:54.047 --> 00:08:56.540
I had $15,000 down,

00:08:56.540 --> 00:09:00.820
and that's 25%, but the people
would not sell it to me.

00:09:00.820 --> 00:09:03.580
So that is a prime example.

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:05.710
But with our society today,

00:09:05.710 --> 00:09:07.920
I don't think you're gonna have a problem.

00:09:07.920 --> 00:09:11.130
They have a lot of super Black artists

00:09:11.130 --> 00:09:14.240
that are incredible tattoo artists,

00:09:14.240 --> 00:09:17.130
and I think that people will come to you

00:09:17.130 --> 00:09:20.123
and appreciate the good
artwork that you're doing.

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:27.840
Once this tattoo is laid out

00:09:27.840 --> 00:09:29.790
and I'm doing free hand,

00:09:29.790 --> 00:09:33.020
I start everything from
the right-hand corner,

00:09:33.020 --> 00:09:34.943
so I don't swipe off my stencil.

00:09:37.820 --> 00:09:38.653
So you'll see me go

00:09:38.653 --> 00:09:40.780
and do a quick outline around
the whole entire thing,

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:42.060
because if I don't,

00:09:42.060 --> 00:09:44.250
it's gonna be wiping off, the
person's gonna be sweatin'

00:09:44.250 --> 00:09:45.240
and all this stuff.

00:09:45.240 --> 00:09:46.520
So I'm doing a quick outline

00:09:46.520 --> 00:09:48.970
around the whole entire
thing so I don't lose it.

00:09:50.433 --> 00:09:52.280
Then I come in with
different line weights,

00:09:52.280 --> 00:09:55.680
doubling up lines to
make it fatter, thicker,

00:09:55.680 --> 00:09:58.960
and then I fill up my
solid black areas next

00:09:58.960 --> 00:10:00.423
to build out contrast.

00:10:01.900 --> 00:10:03.090
When I'm doing color,

00:10:03.090 --> 00:10:06.250
I always say, "What's the
darkest point on my tattoo?"

00:10:06.250 --> 00:10:07.207
So I might do, say,

00:10:07.207 --> 00:10:09.090
"Hey, I'm gonna use turquoise

00:10:09.090 --> 00:10:11.050
and I'm gonna do another
color next to turquoise

00:10:11.050 --> 00:10:12.940
that's not related to turquoise",

00:10:12.940 --> 00:10:15.540
so it's gonna make the
darker turquoise color

00:10:15.540 --> 00:10:17.183
look even brighter.

00:10:19.010 --> 00:10:19.843
With the black

00:10:19.843 --> 00:10:21.860
and grays, there's more
thinking with black

00:10:21.860 --> 00:10:23.490
and gray a little bit, because with black

00:10:23.490 --> 00:10:26.530
and gray, you really gotta
make an image pop out using

00:10:26.530 --> 00:10:27.853
only a few tones.

00:10:29.810 --> 00:10:32.450
With a color tattoo, the black

00:10:32.450 --> 00:10:35.010
and gray under the tattoo
is just your contrast.

00:10:35.010 --> 00:10:35.843
Then you're throwing color

00:10:35.843 --> 00:10:37.810
right on top of that, like a overlay.

00:10:37.810 --> 00:10:39.420
So, those pores are open

00:10:39.420 --> 00:10:41.500
and they're healing at the same time,

00:10:41.500 --> 00:10:44.110
so you gotta hurry up doing this color,

00:10:44.110 --> 00:10:45.100
it's getting swollen

00:10:45.100 --> 00:10:46.720
and sore right now.

00:10:46.720 --> 00:10:49.520
I gotta knock this out before
this person tap out on me.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:11:00.520
I was probably always
an artist growing up.

00:11:00.520 --> 00:11:02.691
My mom was like, I wasn't even walking,

00:11:02.691 --> 00:11:04.343
and I could draw.

00:11:05.320 --> 00:11:09.080
And then as a child, I
was going to the library

00:11:09.080 --> 00:11:11.730
and one of the books I
picked up was created

00:11:11.730 --> 00:11:13.620
by Don Ed Hardy.

00:11:13.620 --> 00:11:17.200
And I read it, I thought about
my father having a tattoo.

00:11:17.200 --> 00:11:19.280
So, my mind clicked.

00:11:19.280 --> 00:11:21.110
I was like "man, you know,

00:11:21.110 --> 00:11:24.147
I think something I'm
interested in doing."

00:11:24.147 --> 00:11:24.980
And you know,

00:11:24.980 --> 00:11:26.720
and in my time wasn't Black
tattoo artists at all.

00:11:26.720 --> 00:11:28.180
It was probably like, at this time,

00:11:28.180 --> 00:11:30.270
probably four in the world,

00:11:30.270 --> 00:11:32.490
and there was no Black tattoo artists

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:35.060
in North Carolina period,
I was the first one.

00:11:35.060 --> 00:11:39.830
So, I ended up going to a tattoo
shop called Liquid Dragon,

00:11:39.830 --> 00:11:40.870
Took me in man

00:11:40.870 --> 00:11:42.700
and just showed me some ropes,

00:11:42.700 --> 00:11:44.130
but I was messing around in the streets

00:11:44.130 --> 00:11:45.510
and kind of blew that.

00:11:45.510 --> 00:11:47.470
Just trying to find myself as a young man,

00:11:47.470 --> 00:11:50.600
getting in a whole bunch of
trouble, like most young men do,

00:11:50.600 --> 00:11:52.540
and I blew my opportunity,

00:11:52.540 --> 00:11:54.630
but it kind of opened up a door for me

00:11:54.630 --> 00:11:55.780
to move out of my hometown

00:11:55.780 --> 00:11:56.780
and move to Atlanta.

00:11:57.730 --> 00:12:01.000
And I ended up opening
up my first tattoo shop.

00:12:01.000 --> 00:12:01.970
It made some good money,

00:12:01.970 --> 00:12:04.130
so it gave me that freedom
to be able to paint

00:12:04.130 --> 00:12:06.273
and explore art more in the gallery scene.

00:12:08.420 --> 00:12:12.058
I'm a painter. That's what
I really do for a living.

00:12:12.058 --> 00:12:14.720
Painting, I feel like
that's Miya right there.

00:12:14.720 --> 00:12:18.960
You get to see pure me. I'm
not worried about nothing.

00:12:18.960 --> 00:12:22.050
I'm not worried about
if it's pretty or not,

00:12:22.050 --> 00:12:23.520
because tattooing, I gotta make it pretty.

00:12:23.520 --> 00:12:24.820
I gotta make it beautiful.

00:12:25.980 --> 00:12:28.433
But painting, you don't
have to make it beautiful.

00:12:29.500 --> 00:12:32.023
You feeling ugly, then
you can express that.

00:12:38.114 --> 00:12:39.114
We are done.

00:12:39.960 --> 00:12:42.280
Once you finished the color, or the black

00:12:42.280 --> 00:12:44.150
and gray, whatever tattoo you're doing,

00:12:44.150 --> 00:12:46.743
the next step is really, they gone see it.

00:12:49.910 --> 00:12:51.230
They look at it from that angle,

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:52.660
but then they wanna look in the mirror

00:12:52.660 --> 00:12:54.370
so they can see up in the reflection

00:12:54.370 --> 00:12:56.420
of how it will be naturally on their arm.

00:12:59.100 --> 00:13:01.120
The second reaction is very important.

00:13:01.120 --> 00:13:02.110
You know what I'm saying?

00:13:02.110 --> 00:13:03.299
'Cause you definitely, it's over,

00:13:03.299 --> 00:13:04.900
you want them to feel relief

00:13:04.900 --> 00:13:07.250
and you want them to be
happy at the same time.

00:13:07.250 --> 00:13:08.083
You like it?

00:13:08.083 --> 00:13:09.122
I love it.

00:13:09.122 --> 00:13:09.955
Gimme a hug.

00:13:09.955 --> 00:13:11.137
Thank you so much,
Thank you.

00:13:11.137 --> 00:13:12.060
it was an honor.

00:13:12.060 --> 00:13:13.890
That's the part I like the most,

00:13:13.890 --> 00:13:15.450
when that client look in that mirror

00:13:15.450 --> 00:13:18.500
and see that permanent
thing I put on them.

00:13:18.500 --> 00:13:21.743
Yeah, man, it's a big responsibility.

00:13:24.430 --> 00:13:26.400
Then we bring them back.

00:13:26.400 --> 00:13:29.343
I take my photos, my
pictures for my social media,

00:13:31.140 --> 00:13:33.380
and then I wrap 'em up.

00:13:33.380 --> 00:13:36.100
I use like a saran wrap, basic stuff,

00:13:36.100 --> 00:13:37.360
just to catch the blood

00:13:37.360 --> 00:13:39.173
and it don't mess up their clothes.

00:13:40.868 --> 00:13:44.063
Thank you so much.
Appreciate it.

00:13:48.300 --> 00:13:51.670
When it's done, I'm more
thankful than anything,

00:13:51.670 --> 00:13:55.210
'cause I'm like, "Damn, I just made $1,500

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:57.200
and this money is gonna
go back to this apprentice

00:13:57.200 --> 00:13:58.033
that's hosting them.

00:13:58.033 --> 00:13:59.530
It's going back to their careers,

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:00.800
giving them a place to work."

00:14:00.800 --> 00:14:03.650
And it's just a good cycle,
that's what I'm trying to do.

00:14:05.170 --> 00:14:07.510
If you want there to be
a vibrant tattoo culture,

00:14:07.510 --> 00:14:09.780
you ought to go be a member of it.

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:11.540
So, go meet tattooers

00:14:11.540 --> 00:14:12.890
and get to know them

00:14:12.890 --> 00:14:14.460
and figure out if you like them,

00:14:14.460 --> 00:14:15.790
'cause you're gonna have a moment

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:17.510
that's gonna be with you for awhile.

00:14:17.510 --> 00:14:20.750
You might as well have a
good time while doing it.

00:14:20.750 --> 00:14:21.720
When it comes to tattooing,

00:14:21.720 --> 00:14:24.133
I be feeling more like a healer or Shaman.

00:14:25.610 --> 00:14:27.930
Even though you really
don't need a tattoo,

00:14:27.930 --> 00:14:30.560
but it made me feel like they needed that,

00:14:30.560 --> 00:14:32.853
and it's a relief for
them, it's a relief for me.

